>
  <p>In 2007 I was writing for DailyCOD, posting blogs on TEK9 and breaking a lot of news in the Call of Duty scene. At some point both MeetYourMakers and Fnatic approached me. I went with Fnatic, partly because of the Dutch heritage in their CS:S team, and partly because Vo0 played for them, a player I'd followed in Dutch gaming magazines growing up. They paid me $200 a month, which mostly went on buying beers for my friends in the pub, who were all stuck in boring supermarket jobs.</p>

  <h2>From writer to Editor-in-Chief</h2>
  <p>I joined as an editor to write about the community and cover Fnatic's own teams, and it quickly grew beyond Call of Duty to every game Fnatic played. In June 2008 I became Editor-in-Chief, running the editorial team for the website and social media. A lot of it happened on IRC, which was where everything lived back then. The milestone I remember is the site passing around 100k registered users.</p>

  <h2>General Manager</h2>
  <p>In 2011 I became General Manager. The main job was the partnership with the streaming platform Own3D. The hard part was getting players to understand their obligations to stream, and the value it would have for them in the long run. Alongside that I was scouting talent, running shop logistics and launching Fnatic's tournament platform, PLAY.</p>

  <h2>Why I left</h2>
  <p>By the summer of 2011 I wanted to experience other things and write on my own platform, and I felt a bit stuck where I was.</p>

  <h2>What I took with me</h2>
  <p>Make yourself expendable. Most people think being indispensable makes you important, but it ties you to your current responsibilities. Delegating, teaching others to do the work and automating what you can frees you up to take the next step.</p>
</CaseStudy>
